Fix Trichrome arm32 V8 torque mismatch.

Root cause: our `v8_snapshot_toolchain = //build/toolchain/linux/unbundle:host`
override (in apks/chromium/default.nix) collapses V8's per-ABI auto-selection
from gni/snapshot_toolchain.gni down to a single host toolchain. That host
toolchain picks up `v8_current_cpu = "arm64"` from our arm64 primary target,
so the torque binary gets compiled with `V8_ENABLE_SANDBOX=1` and
`V8_ENABLE_POINTER_COMPRESSION=1` baked in. Torque then emits
`trusted-object-tq.cc` with `self_indirect_pointer` and a
`static_assert(kSelfIndirectPointerOffset == ...)` line.

Primary (android_clang_arm64) compiles torque's output against C++ headers
that also have V8_ENABLE_SANDBOX=1, so the assert passes. Secondary
(android_clang_arm) compiles those same torque outputs against C++ headers
built with v8_enable_sandbox=false (arm32 can't do pointer compression →
sandbox auto-off), so `kSelfIndirectPointerOffset` is undefined → build error.

Fix: run torque twice — once for arm64 with sandbox on (existing path,
unchanged) and once for arm32 in a second unbundle toolchain that pins
`v8_current_cpu = "arm"`. Each ABI's `run_torque` action now depends on
its matching torque binary, so each `torque-generated/` tree is consistent
with the headers it will be compiled against.

Two files touched:

  * build/toolchain/linux/unbundle/BUILD.gn — add `host_v8_arm`, a clone of
    `host` that forces `v8_current_cpu = "arm"` in toolchain_args. Same
    compiler (nixpkgs host clang via BUILD_CC), just different V8 config.

  * v8/BUILD.gn — route `run_torque("")` to `host_v8_arm` when
    `v8_current_cpu == "arm"`, and extend the `v8_executable("torque")`
    guard so torque also builds in the `host_v8_arm` toolchain context.

--- a/build/toolchain/linux/unbundle/BUILD.gn
+++ b/build/toolchain/linux/unbundle/BUILD.gn
@@ -39,3 +39,26 @@ gcc_toolchain("host") {
     current_os = current_os
   }
 }
+
+# Host-runnable toolchain that forces v8_current_cpu = "arm", used by the
+# patched v8/BUILD.gn to build a second torque binary whose baked V8 config
+# (no sandbox, no pointer compression) matches the arm32 secondary ABI's
+# target compile. See chromium-148-v8-secondary-abi-torque.patch.
+gcc_toolchain("host_v8_arm") {
+  cc = getenv("BUILD_CC")
+  cxx = getenv("BUILD_CXX")
+  ar = getenv("BUILD_AR")
+  nm = getenv("BUILD_NM")
+  ld = cxx
+
+  extra_cflags = getenv("BUILD_CFLAGS")
+  extra_cppflags = getenv("BUILD_CPPFLAGS")
+  extra_cxxflags = getenv("BUILD_CXXFLAGS")
+  extra_ldflags = getenv("BUILD_LDFLAGS")
+
+  toolchain_args = {
+    current_cpu = current_cpu
+    current_os = current_os
+    v8_current_cpu = "arm"
+  }
+}
--- a/v8/BUILD.gn
+++ b/v8/BUILD.gn
@@ -2481,7 +2481,14 @@ template("run_torque") {

 # Default run_torque action
 run_torque("") {
-  toolchain = v8_generator_toolchain
+  # For the arm32 secondary ABI, depend on a torque binary built with
+  # v8_current_cpu="arm" (sandbox/pointer-compression off) so the emitted
+  # torque-generated/ tree matches the headers arm32 V8 is compiled against.
+  if (v8_current_cpu == "arm") {
+    toolchain = "//build/toolchain/linux/unbundle:host_v8_arm"
+  } else {
+    toolchain = v8_generator_toolchain
+  }
 }

 if (v8_verify_torque_generation_invariance) {
@@ -7981,6 +7988,7 @@ if (v8_enable_webassembly) {
   }
 }

-if (current_toolchain == v8_snapshot_toolchain) {
+if (current_toolchain == v8_snapshot_toolchain ||
+    current_toolchain == "//build/toolchain/linux/unbundle:host_v8_arm") {
   v8_executable("torque") {
     visibility = [ ":*" ]  # Only targets in this file can depend on this.

